now to Idaho where there are new details  emerging after police finally arrested a  suspect in the murder of four college  students 28 year old criminology student  Brian coburger was taken into custody  Friday in Pennsylvania the families of  the four victims say the arrest brings  them hope but there are still a lot of  questions Dania Backus has more from  Moscow Idaho  investigators are combing through every  aspect of suspect Brian coburger's life  as they try to piece together a possible  motive in the murders of four University  of Idaho college students we're trying  to build this picture now of  him who he is his history how we got to  this event law enforcement sources say  FBI agents tracked the 28 year olds  movements days leading up to his arrest  at his parents home in eastern  Pennsylvania it's located more than 2500  miles from the house in Moscow Idaho  where coburger is accused of stabbing  Kaley Gonzalez Madison Mogan Zana  kernodle and Ethan Chapin while they  were sleeping on November 13th  Gonzalez's father says their arrest  brings some relief this is the first bit  of joy that we've had in close to seven  weeks we hope they picked the right guy  and that gives us hope and we haven't  had hope for a long time co-burger is a  PhD criminology student at Washington  State University and lived in Pullman  Washington about a 15-minute drive from  the victims  police haven't found a murder weapon or  said if coburger knew the students law  enforcement sources say forensic  analysis allegedly linked him to the  murders  the public defender representing  co-burger in the extradition says about  a month after the murders his client  went on a road trip home to Pennsylvania  with his father that was planned at the  beginning of the semester they were in a  white Hyundai Elantra is the same make  and model of the vehicle spotted near  the scene of the murders the police  department's believes that the person we  have in custody which would be co-worker  is the one who is responsible for these  crimes  in their first statement the family of  Brian coburger said they care deeply for  the four families who have lost children  they also said they are fully  cooperating with police in an attempt to  seek the truth and promote his  presumption of innocence Ed  anyavakis in Idaho Forest thank you
 

